title: Log storage
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: What an operation records, its before and after snapshot rows, why row ids are restored and versions recomputed, and how one write lays it all down.

The log lives in the repository's store, beside the data it describes — one transaction
writes both ([[Store tables]]): a ''revision'' holds its timestamp, label and origin; an
''operation'' the fields below; each operation its before and after ''snapshot'' rows;
HEAD is a record of its own; the restorations a skipped navigation step leaves wait in
their own table and survive a crash. (Watcher events are not here: they are buffered in
memory.) The names below — `operation`, `op_snapshot`, `log_head` — are those of the
SQLite tables of before September 2026.

|!Operation field |!Holds |
|`id` |never reused |
|`parent_id` |the operation it follows; none for the root |
|`rev_id`, `seq` |its revision, and its place in it (from 1) |
|`op_type` |[[Revisions and operations]] |
|`entity_uuid` |the metarecord it changed |
|`entity_version_before`, `…_after` |the metarecord's version on each side — provenance, not authority |
|`field_name` |for a field operation |
|`reverts_op_id` |for an operation a revert wrote: the one it undid |

The store also keeps each operation's children, each revision's operations in order and
each metarecord's operations, so a walk down the tree, a revision's listing and a
metarecord's history are each a prefix read.

`reverts_op_id` is read by displays and by the undo selection — which is why a revert
records it rather than merely looking like one. Nothing on the write path branches on it,
and it may dangle: pruning and retention may remove the operation it names.

!! Snapshots

Each operation stores its state before and after as rows in the column form of a field
row ([[The column form of a value]]), each keeping its original field id:

|!Type |!Before |!After |
|`create_metarecord` |— |every row of the new metarecord |
|`delete_metarecord` |every row of the deleted one |— |
|`set_field` |the previous rows of the name |the new rows |
|`append_field` |— |the appended row |
|`delete_field` |the deleted rows |— |
|`file_deleted` |the `mfr_path` row |one `nothing` row |
|`file_moved` |the previous `mfr_path` row |the new one |
|`file_modified` |`mfr_size`, `mfr_mtime` and the hash rows |the new `mfr_size`, `mfr_mtime` |

A directory's `file_deleted` gives each descendant its own operation in the same revision.

!! Ids restored, versions recomputed

A row id is never reused, and an undone operation puts rows back ''under their original
id'' — so an operation on another branch naming the same row stays valid after a
navigation crosses it. The version is the opposite: never restored from the log but
''recomputed'' from the rows just put back — it is a hash of the content
([[Metarecord version]]), so it is necessarily the version that state had. Carrying it
would only make a second source of truth. The `entity_version_*` fields stay, costing
nothing at write time, for clients correlating an operation with something observed
outside; no navigation reads them to decide what to write.

!! One write

Every request that changes metadata goes through one `log::Writer`, in one transaction:
a revision is opened (with the request's label); for each change, the affected rows and
the version are read (the before snapshot), the change is applied (the version updated
incrementally), the resulting rows read (the after snapshot), and an operation written
whose parent is the running HEAD, which then moves to it; finally HEAD is recorded and
the transaction commits. Every writer — reconcile included — follows it, which is what
keeps the data and HEAD consistent ([[Writes and atomicity]]).

(An `unknown` operation type exists for an unlogged write — no snapshots, a weak root
navigation cannot go before. No endpoint writes one: the `no_log` option it was meant for
was never needed.)
